{"results":[{"url":"https://apnews.com/article/fact-checking-9565471123","final_url":"https://apnews.com/article/fact-checking-9565471123","title":"Kanye West tweet showed mock election data, not results","description":"False. The numbers shared by West on Twitter, purportedly from the Kentucky election, were not real results.","language":"en","text":"By \nARIJETA LAJKA\n\nPublished 12:45 PM EST, October 14, 2020\n\nShare\n\n* Facebook\n* Copy\n\n  Link copied\n* Print\n* Email\n* X\n* LinkedIn\n* Bluesky\n* Flipboard\n* Pinterest\n* Reddit\n\nCLAIM: Election results in Kentucky show rapper and music producer Kanye West ahead of President Donald Trump and Democratic nominee Joe Biden in the presidential race.\n\nAP’S ASSESSMENT: False. The numbers shared by West on Twitter, purportedly from the Kentucky election, were not real results. They were mock election data used by The Associated Press as part of routine testing ahead of elections.\n\nTHE FACTS: On Tuesday, West tweeted a screenshot of bogus election data from Kentucky NBC affiliate’s LEX 18 News. Mock election results showed West had 40,000 votes in Kentucky and was ahead of Trump and Biden. The screenshot showed that West was in third place behind Libertarian Party’s Jo Jorgensen and Brock Pierce, who is running as an independent candidate. The false post had over 11,000 retweets.\n\n“GET THE WEST WING READY !!! ... this is how I felt when I saw that Kentucky pole result,” West tweeted, with a video of him sharing the results on his phone.\n\n“YE 2020 AFTER ALL? Kanye West is leading Joe Biden and Donald Trump in Kentucky precinct voting polls,” read another tweeted sharing the false information. The post had over 2,000 retweets.\n\nThe Kentucky NBC affiliate’s LEX 18 News clarified on Twitter that the results were not real numbers, and were part of a test.\n\n“Someone discovered a cached web link that we used during June’s primary election to post Associated Press election results. The old link was still populating current AP data and showed test results, which is part of the preparation the AP does in advance of elections,” the tweet stated. “The results shown were not valid. They were simply part of a test. We regret the discovery of the cached web link and have removed the data from that page. We apologize for any confusion.”\n\nTwitter labeled the post as “manipulated media.” The false election results also circulated on Instagram.\n\nWest, who once backed Trump, broke with the president and launched his own presidential bid. His campaign filed paperwork on July 15 with the Federal Election Commission. Early in-person voting began on Oct. 13 in Kentucky and runs through Nov. 2.\n\n\\_\\_\n\nThis is part of The Associated Press’ ongoing effort to fact-check misinformation that is shared widely online, including work with Facebook to identify and reduce the circulation of false stories on the platform.\n\nHere’s more information on Facebook’s fact-checking program: https://www.facebook.com/help/1952307158131536","author":null,"published_date":"2020-10-14T16:45:10","latency_ms":23534.43897899706,"format":"markdown"}],"errors":[]}